\cf3 > \cf4 When I'm on the catalog page, it doesn't show that I'm logged in, even though I am logged in on the homepage, shopping cart, and dashboard. why?\cf2 \
\
\cf3 \uc0\u10022  \cf2 The "Catalog" page (\cf3 /courses\cf2 ) is managed by \cf3 CourseController::index\cf2 .\
\
  
\f1\b Root Cause Analysis:
\f0\b0 \
   1. 
\f1\b Session Start
\f0\b0 : Unlike \cf3 HomeController\cf2 , the \cf3 CourseController\cf2  likely doesn't check if the session is started in its \cf3 index\cf2  method (or constructor).\
   2. 
\f1\b View Logic
\f0\b0 : The \cf3 resources/views/layouts/public.php\cf2  layout relies on \cf3 $_SESSION['user_id']\cf2  being set to show the "Dashboard" button instead of "Login".\
   3. 
\f1\b Controller Code
\f0\b0 :\
\
   \cf4 1 \cf2     \cf8 public\cf2  \cf8 function\cf2  index(): void\
   \cf4 2 \cf2     \{\
   \cf4 3 \cf2         \cf3 $courseModel\cf2  = \cf8 new\cf2  Course();\
   \cf4 4 \cf2         \cf4 // ... fetching logic ...\cf2 \
   \cf4 5 \cf2         \cf3 $this\cf2 ->view(\cf7 'courses.index'\cf2 , [\cf7 'courses'\cf2  => \cf3 $courses\cf2 ]);\
   \cf4 6 \cf2     \}\
      If \cf3 session_start()\cf2  wasn't called before this point, \cf3 $_SESSION\cf2  is empty in the view.\
\
  
\f1\b Fix:
\f0\b0 \
  I need to add \cf3 session_start()\cf2  to the \cf3 __construct\cf2  method of \cf3 CourseController\cf2 , or specifically in the \cf3 index\cf2  method if we want to be granular. A constructor approach is safer for all\
  methods in that controller.\
\
  I will update \cf3 App\\Controllers\\CourseController.php\cf2 .\
